Post Date: 2018-10-12T00:49:55Z
Current Date: April 4 2025
Title: Does not work installed signal if in swoole_process run a sleep(1) function
Description: Hi,

I wrote a simple daemon program that starts up a few subprocesses. I also set up a signal handler for the master and child processes with swoole::signal(). If I use sleep(1) within the child process, the child process does not respond to the signal given by swoole::kill(). usleep (1000000) function does not have this problem.

// $worker is master process
```php 
        $process = new swoole_process(function(swoole_process $process) use($worker, $id){
            
            if (self::$status === self::STATUS_STARTING) {
                self::resetStd();
            }
            
            self::$pidMap  = array();
            self::$workers = array($worker->workerId => $worker);
            Timer::delAll();
            
            $process->name('Messenger: worker process  ' . $worker->name);
            $worker->setUserAndGroup();
            $worker->id = $id;
            
            self::$status = self::STATUS_RUNNING;
        
            register_shutdown_function(array("\\Jetmail\\Server\\Messenger", 'checkErrors'));
            self::reinstallSignal();
            
            // Init Timer.
            Timer::init();
            
            $timerid = Timer::add(3,function()use($process,&$timerid){
                
                while($msg = $process->pop()){
                    if ($msg === false) {
                        break;
                    }

                    sleep(1);
                    //usleep(1000000);
                }
            },[1,2,3],true);            
        });
        
        $process->useQueue(rand(1,1000), swoole_process::IPC_NOWAIT);
        $pid = $process->start();
```


I do not know this is a bug or my knowledge is incomplete. I applied sleep (1) instead of a specific task until I did the daemon program. Program execution is waiting for 1 second to simulate the execution time of the real task

Subprocess's reactions to the signal were never responded, and there was a program state when child processes ceased as soon as they received a signal. I always sent the SIGUSR2 signal.


